The resolver now rejects instantiating a type parameter whose values may be stored in memory with a type that contains a function that reads the heap (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/481). A generic class could store a value of its type parameter in a mutable field; instantiated with `() ~> bool`, the stored function could read itself, and a program proved `false`. The existing check sees only the static type of the stored value, which in generic code is the type parameter. A type parameter may be stored if it occurs in the type of a mutable field of its class, trait or iterator, if a value whose type mentions it is stored in a mutable field or an array element, or put in a new array by an initializer, or if it is passed to a type parameter that may be stored. The check also holds for clients in other modules, through export sets and abstract imports that hide bodies, and for libraries given as `.doo` files. An overriding member, or a declaration in a refining module, may store a type parameter only if the member it overrides, or the declaration it refines, may. A base without a body that must allow such a store declares its type parameter with the new type characteristic `(!reads)`. The hidden option `--legacy-generic-function-stores` restores the old, unsound rule, for migration and comparison only.
